      "notes": "Verdict is well-constructed and passes all checks. Claims are supported by their quoted evidence: E23 supports the urban-skew measurable claim; E7 supports the rural improvement figures; E18 supports the 2 million affordability barrier (though sourced from ucadvanced.com, a media outlet, the claim is presented as an estimate and is not the sole basis for the verdict); E19 correctly characterises 'encourages' as voluntary; E27 uses 'potentially' language matching the projected/uncertain nature \u2014 though labelled measurable, the quote itself says 'potentially due to' which is hedged, a minor concern but not blocking since the core claim (lower take-up rates) is factual; E14 supports the land-value projection without overstating; E20 supports the digital-exclusion risk claim accurately. Tier integrity is sound: projections are labelled projected. The land-value claim (E14) is correctly projected. The 'mixed' direction is genuinely supported by claims on both sides \u2014 regional narrowing vs affordability/wealth-gap widening \u2014 so no false_balance issue. No party identity or motive language appears. Magnitude 'minor' and 'long-term' horizon are plausible given the weak distributional instruments. One minor note: E27 is backed solely by broadbandfreedom.co.uk (media/commercial-adjacent), and the claim is labelled measurable \u2014 this is a warning-level concern but the claim is modest and hedged. No lever leakage detected; the verdict scores distributional effects on inequality, not broadband policy for its own sake."
    },
